PASS V8 No page errors while completing every season-3 session
PASS C1 After the bootloader (seasons 1–2) the compiler scores the remaining sessions from capability gaps, errors, unpossessed works, rotation and dependants, and says why
PASS C2 Trial L10: one compiled day in about five keeps the written order as a control; the rest follow the compiler
PASS C3 Deep sessions (the trial, a boss world) are compiled only on the weekend, with a 45-minute dose; on a weekday the door says it waits for the weekend
PASS C4 A started session always continues first; nothing is compiled in the middle of one
PASS C5 Every session of every season compiles to the full session object (§161): capabilities, sources, irreducible experiences, tasks, misconception branches, transfer, forge, hooks, minutes, stop condition, rights
PASS E1 Trials (L2 step order, L3 step length, L4 session length, L5 hints, L6 spacing, L7 task type, L8 source mix, L9 session architecture, L10 season order, L11 capability priority) each have a control arm, a minimum sample, a success margin and a no-harm floor; the judge adopts a clearly better arm, drops one that is not better, and stops one below the floor
PASS E2 Trial L2 really changes the lesson: some sessions play the two compared cases before the model and others keep the written order, by a stable per-session assignment
PASS E3 Rollback: renaissance_experiments = "off" returns every trial to the written lesson at once
PASS E4 Level 14 (the judge judged): an adopted arm that later does worse than its monitoring control is rolled back, and that trial then needs a larger sample (caution ×1.5)
PASS E5 No page errors in the trial checks
PASS P1 Day 0: the baseline form opens the first day (two sealed items before the session), unlabelled, with every helper off
PASS P2 A form item gives no right/wrong feedback (it returns at day 90), and the stored record keeps only its id and outcome, never its words
PASS P3 Schedule: this week's unknown problem arrives on day 7; the parallel form B on day 30; nothing in exam week (short dose); a window missed by more than two weeks is skipped, not owed
PASS P4 A weekly unknown problem is used once, so it explains itself after you commit; results feed the orientation dimension of the capability vector, never the in-session rates
PASS P5 Reader-Turing questions for The Brothers Karamazov arrive 30 days after its last session, and not before
PASS P6 No page errors in the sealed-measurement checks
PASS S1 Pre-registration: all 52 sealed items (8 baseline, 8 thirty-day, 24 weekly unknowns, 12 reader questions) match the SHA-256 registered before any answer; any later edit fails here
PASS S2 Leak firewall: no run of six words from any sealed item appears anywhere in the lessons, hooks, visuals, models or quotation register; the sealed file carries no item text in the clear (not in content, metadata, file name or order)
PASS S3 Counterfeit learners, extended (§152–153): longest, shortest, most words, punctuation, hedges, jargon, no absolutes, overlap with the question, odd length, most typical option, positive words, first shown, last shown: each stays within 0.12 of chance in every season and on the sealed items
PASS L1 Functional-capability vector (§38): separate dimensions, each with its n and no composite score; rates are hidden below five answers (no fake precision)
PASS L2 Transformation velocity (§45–46): the weekly slope of delayed, unaided accuracy is computed with its weeks and n; acceleration only once six such weeks exist
PASS L3 Multiplex (§36–37): only capability atoms with a later or unaided success count, per hour; tags alone never do
PASS L4 Cultural-possession ladder (§162): each rung is earned by evidence (a read passage counts only at reading speed), a rung counts only if the rungs below it are earned, and the top rung needs the sealed reader questions
PASS L5 The twin (§163) tracks each idea from seen to assimilated, and flags what went backwards
PASS D1 Device independence: the whole record exports as one JSON (renaissance.state/1) and imports on another device, merging sessions and answers; a foreign file is refused
PASS D2 Voice-ready: the current step reads as plain speech (question, lettered options; a passage with its speaker and source), with no markup, for earbuds or a screen reader
PASS D3 Failure recovery (§247): an unreadable record is kept aside unchanged, a clean one starts, the organ still opens, and WHY THIS? says what happened
PASS D4 Garbage collection of drills (§101–103): after three sure recalls, an idea whose next gap would pass half a year retires as assimilated and is not asked again
PASS D5 No page errors in the learner-model and device checks
PASS Q1 No-slop reviewer (§15, §139, §220): no generic theme-talk, vague praise or AI filler in any teaching text (allowed only inside a wrong option diagnosed as pretension); no repeated step text; every figure is used and says what it shows
PASS A1 Accessibility (§158): body text, buttons and labels meet WCAG AA contrast (4.5:1); every control is a native button or input (keyboard-operable); the organ is a labelled dialog; Enter on a focused CONTINUE advances
PASS A2 Performance (§159): opening a session takes under 300 ms and every model redraws in under 60 ms (the slowest is named)
PASS A3 No page errors in the review, accessibility and performance checks
PASS G1 Capability genome (§60–62): all 28 atoms carry components, prerequisites, synergies, conflicts, transfer edges, expressions and descendants; evidence and learning cost are computed; references resolve; prerequisites have no cycle; the periodic table places each atom once and every empty cell names a predicted atom; ≥ 7 compounds
PASS G2 Genome evidence comes from the record: 5 of 6 unaided answers gives 0.83; below five answers the rate is withheld; untouched atoms show none
PASS M1 Media registry (§134–139): every visual, model, listening passage, quotation and data object has a §138 type, a §134 rights class, a source and a stated cognitive job, and is used by a step; quotations carry §135 provenance; every visual has an accessible name
PASS F1 Reality calibration (§97): after building, the learner can forecast (optional) whether they will try it; the next warm-up checks it, scores it and says so; after five checked forecasts a Brier score is reported (0.234 for the fixture), none before
PASS F2 No page errors in the genome, media and calibration checks
PASS V9 Masterpiece experience quality (§217): the Karamazov track has primary text, a structural map, a character graph and model, vocabulary, context, criticism, quotations in context, transfer, discussion, memory hooks and sealed reader-Turing questions
PASS V10 Idea immune system and source conflict (§93, §96, §99, §137): every session meets a counterexample, a stated limit or an open question; contested claims are marked contested; opposing verdicts (Freud, Nabokov) are shown side by side; at least five sessions keep rival models alive
PASS V11 Texture, not trivia (§227–229): no step dumps more than two quotations, no quotation is shown in more than two steps, every quotation carries its context and why it matters, prose quotations stay under 160 words, and no returning question asks for a bare date or name
PASS E6 The trials beyond L6 change the lesson on their test arm and not on their control: L4 splits a session over two days (and says so), L7 hides the options until an answer is in mind, L8 puts the context beside the passage, L9 moves returning ideas after the session, L11 weighs rotation above gaps
PASS L6 Personal intellectual physics (§74–75): a rule about this learner appears only with enough evidence (8+ per kind) and says how it would be refuted; below that there is no rule
PASS L7 Museum and concert tests (§31–32): cold predictions against later answers on new works for the art and music sessions, with the change; rates only from five answers
PASS L8 World-model map (§98–99): all sixteen layers of §98 are listed with the sessions that model them and what is done; empty layers are named as gaps, never hidden; rival models kept are listed
PASS F3 Real social feedback (§232): after the conversation session the next warm-up asks whether it came up in real talk (went well, showed a gap, raised a question, did not come up); only the tap and its time are kept
PASS C6 Sovereignty (§197): on a compiled day WHY THIS? offers the runners-up; choosing one replaces the compiler's pick before any answer, leaves the abandoned session untouched, and is recorded as his choice
PASS F5 Click events (§84): a prediction that was wrong before the explanation, then the idea used rightly on an unseen case, is logged as a click for that session
PASS F4 No page errors in the masterpiece, immune-system, trial and learner-model checks
